# Modeling discipline

The point of this project is honest evaluation, not high reported accuracy. A model that
looks worse but is measured correctly is strictly more valuable than one that looks better
because of a subtle leak.

## Feature rules

- Signature is always `f(entity_id, as_of) -> Value | Unknown`. No exceptions.
- Rolling windows use **prior games only**. Handle byes, short weeks, and missed games
  explicitly — never silently forward-fill.
- Injury and depth-chart features use the last report **before** kickoff, not final status.
- Odds features use a declared snapshot policy (e.g. T-60min). **The closing line is never a
  feature** — it is reserved for CLV evaluation.
- Feature rows are stored with the `as_of` they were computed for, so a backtest replays the
  exact vector the model saw.
- Every feature is registered with its as-of semantics and source. An unregistered feature
  cannot enter a model.

## Evaluation rules

- **Time-aware splits only.** Walk-forward / expanding window by season and week. Random
  k-fold on game data is a bug, not a choice.
- **Three mandatory baselines** must be beaten before claiming anything: market-implied
  probability, season-to-date average, and last-N average.
- Always report: log loss, Brier score, calibration curve, and performance by confidence
  bucket. Accuracy alone is not a result.
- For betting-oriented models also report: implied vs. model probability, edge distribution,
  hit rate, ROI, max drawdown, and **performance against the closing line**.
- **Accuracy and profitability are separate claims.** Never present one as evidence of the
  other. A well-calibrated model can have zero edge if the market prices the same
  information.
- Results are written to tables, not pasted into notebooks or chat. Every reported number is
  reproducible from a stored artifact plus a model version.

## Predictions

Every stored prediction carries: model version, feature-set version, `as_of` timestamp, and
the run ID that produced it. A prediction without provenance is deleted, not debugged.

## Red flags — stop and flag rather than proceed

- A feature that improves out-of-sample performance dramatically and suddenly. Suspect a
  leak first, celebrate second.
- Any model beating the closing line by a wide margin on backtest. Almost always leakage or
  a stale-line comparison.
- Reported ROI computed against opening lines you would not actually have been able to bet.
